-- glsl SquarebitEye.Core

float ec_saturate(float x) { return clamp(x, 0.0f, 1.0f); }

// The SHIPPED iris radius (gen_materialx.SCALARS LimbusRadius) and the
// height of its limbus rim on the unit eyeball, sqrt(1 - L^2). These are
// the REFERENCE the iris plane is quoted against, so the authored
// irisPlaneZ means exactly what it says on the default eye and scales from
// there. tools/check_bulge_mirrors.py gates them against SCALARS - a
// reference that drifts from the default it refers to is silently wrong.
// The anatomical corneal index, and the fraction of the limbus radius that
// the head-on limbus ray lands at on the SHIPPED eye. That fraction IS the
// refraction behaviour - how much of its aperture the iris fills - and
// ecCorneaIor holds it constant as the iris moves. Both are gated against
// SCALARS by tools/check_bulge_mirrors.py.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// LOCAL EDIT, and the only one: the eye reads its own OBJECT space.
//
// As generated this shader projects against a unit eyeball at the WORLD
// origin looking down +Z -- the centre and forward are literals in the
// eye_project call below, because Storm cannot hand material parameters
// to the geometry stage. That is exact for the reference stage, whose
// eye is at the origin, and wrong for an eye in a head: ours sits at
// (3.38, 164.30, 1.38), so every fragment projected far outside the
// iris and the eye shaded as plain sclera.
//
// Dividing out the prim's own transform puts the fragment back in the
// space the literals describe. HD_HAS_transform is Storm's guard for a
// prim that has one; without it this is the identity and the generated
// behaviour is untouched, so the reference stage still renders exactly
// as it did.
//
// Uniform scale and a translation is all the eye carries, so the normal
// needs no inverse-transpose -- transform and renormalise is exact here.
//
// IN CORE, not in Surface. The configuration builds the surface shader
// from Core + Surface and the displacement shader from Core +
// Displacement, and both call this. Declared in Surface it is missing
// from the displacement compile, that shader fails, and Storm drops the
// whole material and draws its fallback -- which is a white eye that
// looks exactly like a shader working on the wrong geometry.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
mat4 ecWorldToObject()
{
    // The PROJECTOR first, when the rig publishes one.
    //
    // The eyeball is moved by a skin and a socket-stretch cluster, so its
    // transform is its placement and cannot also be the frame the iris is
    // quoted against. The rig computes the projector separately and hands
    // it over as a primvar, which is the only channel a per-evaluation
    // matrix has into the geometry stage.
    //
    // The cast is not cosmetic: where the device reports double
    // precision Storm uploads a matrix4d primvar as dmat4, and assigning
    // that to a mat4 fails to compile -- which drops the whole material
    // to the grey fallback and reports nothing.
    //
    // Falling back to the prim's transform keeps every stage that has no
    // rig -- the eye's own reference stage among them -- as it was.
#if defined(HD_HAS_eyeProjector)
    return inverse(mat4(HdGet_eyeProjector()));
#elif defined(HD_HAS_transform)
    return inverse(HdGet_transform());
#else
    return mat4(1.0);
#endif
}



